## The `HolRefute` Library

\label{sec:HolRefute}
\index{HolRefute|(}
\index{counterexamples|see {HolRefute}}
\index{model finding|see {HolRefute}}

`HolRefute` searches for finite counterexamples to HOL conjectures.  It
combines Quickcheck-style execution, native narrowing, and a
Kodkod-backed finite model finder.  It is intended for finding mistakes
quickly, while a proof is being developed; a report that no
counterexample was found within the selected bounds is not a proof.

The interface is a family of diagnostic tactics.  A tactic prints what
the search found and leaves the goal unchanged, so it can be dropped in
front of the real proof, read, and left in place or removed.  The SML
entry points behind the tactics are described in
"The SML interface" below; tools that consume structured results need
them, ordinary proof scripts do not.

### Getting started

The library is available on Poly/ML builds.  In a theory script, name it
in the `Libs` declaration:

```sml
Theory myTheory
Libs
  Refute
```

For an interactive session, load the public facade and open it:

    load "Refute"; open Refute;

This loads the implementation modules and registers the built-in
backends and generators.  Three of the four backends are native and need
no external software; the `kodkod` model finder needs a separately
installed component, described under "Installing Kodkodi" below.

Loading and running Refute leaves no Refute-created type, constant,
theorem, or theory binding in the user's theory.  In particular,
temporary compilation artefacts are removed on success, failure,
timeout, and interruption.  Loading Refute does have one deliberate
ambient effect: it extends the global `EVAL` compset so that closed
`:rat` and `:real` literals compute afterwards, whether or not Refute
itself is invoked again.  "Types the executable backends can test"
below gives the details.

### The diagnostic tactics

| Tactic | Search |
|---|---|
| `REFUTE_TAC` | Every backend selected by the current configuration. |
| `QUICKCHECK_TAC` | Exhaustive, random, and narrowing search. |
| `NARROWING_TAC` | Symbolic narrowing only. |
| `MODEL_REFUTE_TAC` | The relational model finder only. |

`REFUTE_TAC` is the normal first choice.  Use one of the other three
only when the search method or the shape of the counterexample matters:
narrowing builds partially known values and accepts prenex existentials,
and the model finder can assign a table to an uninterpreted function,
which executable testing cannot.

All four print an outcome and then behave as `ALL_TAC`.  They never
prove, admit, or alter the goal, and a counterexample is not reported as
tactic failure.  The normal proof-script idiom therefore keeps the real
proof after the diagnostic:

```sml
Theorem tail_is_shorter:
  xs <> [] ==> LENGTH (TL (xs : num list)) < LENGTH xs
Proof
  REFUTE_TAC >>
  Cases_on `xs` >> simp []
QED
```

While a conjecture is still being repaired, the tactic is run on its
own: inspect the report, fix the statement, and try again.

The following is a static transcript from a Refute-enabled session.
Timing fields are machine-dependent, and reports are re-wrapped for the
page: an interactive session prints each one as a single
`<<HOL message: ...>>` block.  The configuration selects the portable
`Compute` execution substrate to make the example independent of an
external compiler, and sequential execution so that the report names one
predictable backend.

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- the_config :=
    (default_config
       |> upd_sequential true
       |> upd_substrate Compute
       |> upd_size 4);
> val it = () : unit

- g `(n : num) < 3`;
(* goal display omitted *)

- e REFUTE_TAC;
> Refute found a counterexample
    (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 3, 0.0s,
     candidates generated 9, assumptions satisfied 9,
     conclusions evaluated 9):
    n = 3
  Certified: |- ~!n. n < 3
(* the goal is returned unchanged *)
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - the_config :=
        (default_config
           |> upd_sequential true
           |> upd_substrate Compute
           |> upd_size 4);
    > val it = () : unit

    - g `(n : num) < 3`;
    (* goal display omitted *)

    - e REFUTE_TAC;
    > Refute found a counterexample
        (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 3, 0.0s,
         candidates generated 9, assumptions satisfied 9,
         conclusions evaluated 9):
        n = 3
      Certified: ⊢ ¬∀n. n < 3
    (* the goal is returned unchanged *)
```

Free variables of the goal are implicitly universally closed, so `n`
above is searched over as if the goal read `!n. n < 3`.  A goal's
assumptions are part of the search problem: Refute looks for values that
make every assumption true and the conclusion false.  `upd_no_assms
true` ignores them instead.

### Reading a report

A counterexample report opens with the backend that found it and the
substrate that executed it, then the bounds and cost of the search, and
then one line per bound free variable.  On a Quickcheck backend
(`exhaustive`, `random`, `narrowing`) the cost is three counts: how many
candidates the search generated, how many of those had every
`Guard`/`SmartGuard`/`Split`/`Bind` premise satisfied, and how many of
those went on to have their conclusion evaluated, printed as "candidates
generated", "assumptions satisfied", and "conclusions evaluated".  The
model finder prints a `Scope:` line recording its exact finite
interpretation instead.

The last line records certification.  `Certified: |- ...` means Refute
constructed a HOL theorem for the negated, closed instance;
`Certification: uncertified` means it did not.  See "Certification and
certainty" below for what each does and does not claim.

A search that covers the whole relevant space says so:

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- the_config :=
    (default_config
       |> upd_sequential true
       |> upd_substrate Compute
       |> upd_search (Only [Exhaustive])
       |> upd_size 4);
> val it = () : unit

- g `!p q : bool. p /\ q ==> p \/ q`;
(* goal display omitted *)

- e REFUTE_TAC;
> Refute searched the whole space: no counterexample is possible
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - the_config :=
        (default_config
           |> upd_sequential true
           |> upd_substrate Compute
           |> upd_search (Only [Exhaustive])
           |> upd_size 4);
    > val it = () : unit

    - g `!p q : bool. p /\ q ==> p \/ q`;
    (* goal display omitted *)

    - e REFUTE_TAC;
    > Refute searched the whole space: no counterexample is possible
```

This is the `NoCounterexample` outcome: no counterexample is possible at
any larger size.  It is a decisive diagnostic claim and stops the other
searches, but it is not a HOL proof object.  A clean fixed-bound search
that does *not* cover every relevant type is inconclusive instead, with
the tested scope frontier among its reasons.

An inconclusive report lists why each backend stopped.  With no witness
there is no cost line in the report proper; the same three counts are
printed separately, one line per backend that ran, on the `"Refute"`
trace at level 2.  They are a diagnostic rather than part of the
verdict, so the default level 1 does not show them.  The following
transcript is `!n. n * n = 7 ==> n < 3` at size 3: no premise-inverting
generator exists for multiplication, so every candidate is really
generated and really rejected, on every backend that measures the
counters at all.

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- set_trace "Refute" 2;
> val it = () : unit

- the_config :=
    (default_config
       |> upd_sequential true
       |> upd_substrate Compute
       |> upd_size 3);
> val it = () : unit

- g `!n : num. n * n = 7 ==> n < 3`;
(* goal display omitted *)

- e QUICKCHECK_TAC;
> exhaustive: candidates generated 9, assumptions satisfied 0,
  conclusions evaluated 0
  random: candidates generated 300, assumptions satisfied 0,
  conclusions evaluated 0
  narrowing: candidates generated 0, assumptions satisfied 0,
  conclusions evaluated 0
  Refute search was inconclusive
  Reasons:
    exhaustive: search space not exhausted
    exhaustive: searched up to size 3
    random: random search exhausted
    random: searched up to size 3
    narrowing: narrowing search exhausted
    narrowing: substrate does not accept this problem
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - set_trace "Refute" 2;
    > val it = () : unit

    - the_config :=
        (default_config
           |> upd_sequential true
           |> upd_substrate Compute
           |> upd_size 3);
    > val it = () : unit

    - g `!n : num. n * n = 7 ==> n < 3`;
    (* goal display omitted *)

    - e QUICKCHECK_TAC;
    > exhaustive: candidates generated 9, assumptions satisfied 0,
      conclusions evaluated 0
      random: candidates generated 300, assumptions satisfied 0,
      conclusions evaluated 0
      narrowing: candidates generated 0, assumptions satisfied 0,
      conclusions evaluated 0
      Refute search was inconclusive
      Reasons:
        exhaustive: search space not exhausted
        exhaustive: searched up to size 3
        random: random search exhausted
        random: searched up to size 3
        narrowing: narrowing search exhausted
        narrowing: substrate does not accept this problem
```

Narrowing reports "substrate does not accept this problem": it declines
a bare arithmetic equation outright, so its counters are the vacuous
`(0, 0, 0)` of a backend that never ran a candidate, not evidence of a
rejected search.  `random`'s 300 (100 candidates per size, sizes 1..3)
is the same cumulative-schedule convention as `exhaustive`'s 9 (2 + 3 +
4): the total counts a schedule's work across every entry, not distinct
candidates, and a domain far smaller than 300 is unsurprising.

A nonzero "assumptions satisfied" already shows a witness-less search
tested real candidates against a real conclusion, so the numerator alone
tells a real search from a vacuous one.  The denominator earns its keep
when the numerator is zero on both sides: measured at size 3, `Compute`,
exhaustive, `!n. n * n = 7 ==> n < 3` reports "candidates generated 9,
assumptions satisfied 0" -- real candidates were generated and every one
was rejected by the premise -- while `!n. n > 100 ==> n < 3` reports
"candidates generated 0, assumptions satisfied 0", because the planner
inverts `n > 100` into a smart generator whose enumeration is empty at
every size, so nothing was generated at all.  Only "candidates
generated" tells those two zero-numerator runs apart.  `Compute` and
`NativeSML` count every candidate for real; `Cv` runs its search as one
compiled call with no room for incremental counting, so it always omits
all three counts rather than fabricate them, and a witness-less `Cv`
search prints no candidate-count line at all rather than announce a
denominator it cannot produce.

Refute reports through the `"Refute"` trace at level 1.  Higher trace
levels show backend and substrate selection, schedules, and compiled
plans; level 0 suppresses normal Refute output.  `upd_quiet true` is the
preferred per-call way to suppress all messages and warnings.

### Configuring a single call

Every tactic reads the interactive configuration `the_config` when it is
applied.  `show_config ()` prints its complete current value.  Mutating
`the_config` changes persistent interactive defaults, which is what the
transcripts above do; for a one-off call, prefer supplying updates
locally:

```sml
REFUTE_TAC_WITH
  [upd_search (Only [Narrowing]),
   upd_size 7,
   upd_timeout 5.0]
```

The updates have type `config -> config` and are applied to
`!the_config` from left to right.  Thus the last update of a field wins.
The global configuration is read when the tactic is applied, not when it
is constructed.  The tactic honours every resulting field, including
`quiet` and `expect`.

For reproducible tools and regression tests, use an exact configuration
that does not depend on the session's defaults at all:

```sml
REFUTE_CONFIG_TAC
  (default_config
     |> upd_search (Only [Narrowing])
     |> upd_size 7)
```

`default_config` contains `default_qc_config` and `default_mf_config`.
`upd_qc` and `upd_mf` replace a complete sub-record; the individual
updaters are generally less brittle.  The full field list is in
"Configuration reference" below.

Expectations are `ExpectNone`, `ExpectUnknown`, `ExpectCex`,
`ExpectModel`, `ExpectNoModel`, `ExpectGenuine`, `ExpectQuasiGenuine`,
and `ExpectPotential`.  A mismatch raises a HOL error.  They are
intended mainly for tests: `upd_expect` is the one field that can make a
diagnostic tactic fail.

### Backends and how they are chosen

Four backends are registered by default, in increasing weight order:

| Weight | Backend | Search |
|---:|---|---|
| 20 | `exhaustive` | Size-bounded enumeration and evaluation. |
| 30 | `random` | Reproducible random generation and evaluation. |
| 40 | `narrowing` | Native symbolic narrowing, including prenex existentials. |
| 50 | `kodkod` | Relational finite-model search through Kodkodi. |

The built-in backend names are represented by a typed interface:

```sml
datatype backend_choice =
    Exhaustive
  | Random
  | Narrowing
  | ModelFinder
  | RegisteredBackend of string

datatype search =
    AllBackends
  | QuickcheckBackends
  | Only of backend_choice list
```

`upd_search` converts a `search` into a configuration updater.
`upd_search AllBackends` selects every configured backend;
`QuickcheckBackends` selects exhaustive, random, and narrowing search;
and `Only choices` selects exactly its nonempty list.  `Only []` is
rejected.  `RegisteredBackend name` selects an extension backend by its
registered name.  Kodkod is absent from an all-backend run when its
external component is not available.  The three specialized tactics are
exactly `REFUTE_TAC_WITH [upd_search ...]` for the corresponding
selection, so `QUICKCHECK_TAC` and `REFUTE_TAC_WITH [upd_search
QuickcheckBackends]` are the same tactic.

Weights order sequential execution and break ties between results of
equal certainty; in parallel mode they do not promise which process
finishes first.  The default `sequential = false` races the selected
backends.  A result stops the race only when its certainty reaches the
best ceiling declared by every eligible backend.  Thus a quick potential
counterexample does not hide a genuine result that another selected
backend can still produce.  A backend registered through
`register_backend` conservatively declares the ceiling `Genuine`; a
backend with a tighter ceiling can use
`register_backend_with_ceiling`.

One call owns one absolute search deadline of `timeout` seconds.
Preprocessing, backend admission, compilation, testing, and solver calls
all consume the same remainder; mandatory cleanup may finish slightly
after it.

#### Execution substrates

Exhaustive and random testing compile a common test-plan language onto
one of three substrates:

* `NativeSML` extracts and compiles the executable fragment in process.
  It is the preferred fast substrate on Poly/ML.
* `Cv` compiles a fuel-bounded HOL test loop and evaluates its
  `cv_compute` translation.  It accepts a smaller, first-order fragment.
* `Compute` interprets plans with `computeLib`.  It is the compatibility
  substrate and supports registered custom and abstract generators.

The default `Auto` tries these in priority order: Native SML, Cv, then
Compute.  It falls through only when a substrate says it is
inapplicable.  Selecting `NativeSML`, `Cv`, or `Compute` explicitly
never silently changes substrate; inapplicability then contributes an
inconclusive reason.  For a fixed `seed`, all three consume the same
specified 64-bit random stream, so a seeded run reproduces the same
candidates whichever substrate ran it.

#### Narrowing

Narrowing is a native backend rather than another choice in this
substrate ladder.  It searches refinement depths upward from 0: a fixed
`size` is its final depth, while the default iterative mode keeps
deepening past the initial window until the deadline.
Purely universal problems use plain narrowing; a prenex problem with an
existential uses the PNF engine when `allow_existentials` is true.
Function inputs are represented by finite update chains when
`finite_functions` is true, with constant functions used for
higher-order domains.  Partially known values are printed with `_` holes.

#### The finite model finder

The model finder translates the original polymorphic problem into a
bounded relational formula.  It handles finite scopes, datatypes and
codatatypes, functions, sets, inductive and coinductive predicates,
typedefs, quotients, binary integers, machine words, characters, finite
maps, and the registered `rat` and `real` fraction encodings, all on by
default.  Its `Scope:` line records the exact finite interpretation.
For example:

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- the_config :=
    (default_config
       |> upd_sequential true
       |> upd_card [(NONE, [2])]);
> val it = () : unit

- g `(x : 'a) = y`;
(* goal display omitted *)

- e MODEL_REFUTE_TAC;
> Refute found a counterexample
    (backend: kodkod, substrate: kodkod, 0.239s):
  Scope: card alpha = 2
    x = a2
    y = a1
  Certified: |- ~!x y. x = y
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - the_config :=
        (default_config
           |> upd_sequential true
           |> upd_card [(NONE, [2])]);
    > val it = () : unit

    - g `(x : 'a) = y`;
    (* goal display omitted *)

    - e MODEL_REFUTE_TAC;
    > Refute found a counterexample
        (backend: kodkod, substrate: kodkod, 0.239s):
      Scope: card α = 2
        x = a2
        y = a1
      Certified: ⊢ ¬∀x y. x = y
```

Kodkod with `upd_falsify false` searches for a model of the conjecture
rather than against it, and reports `Model` or `NoModel` instead.
Backend selection never changes the meaning of a counterexample or of
`NoCounterexample`.  Model search is auxiliary in a mixed backend run,
so a decisive counterexample result can preempt it.

A word type of concrete width `w` is a native carrier of exactly `2^w`
atoms rather than a datatype or a typedef, and models print surface word
literals.  Word literals, `n2w`, `w2n`, the arithmetic and bitwise
operations, the shifts, and the unsigned and signed orders are encoded
directly.  Width-changing and bit-permuting operations, such as `w2w`,
`word_concat`, the rotations, and `word_reverse`, are refused by name
instead of approximated, so an inconclusive report never passes for a
proof.  The carrier is exact and never searched, so no scope sizes it,
but it must be materialised: a width whose carrier would exceed 1024
atoms is refused by name, which in practice caps the model finder at
width 10 even though `upd_widths` accepts entries up to 32.  A width
left as a type variable has no carrier at all; such goals are reached
through the monomorphic width instances that `upd_widths` selects, which
the executable backends test as well.

`:char` is the same kind of carrier: exactly 256 atoms, atom `j`
denoting `CHR j`, exact and never searched, with models printed as
character literals.  Character literals, `CHR`, `ORD`, and `char_lt`,
`char_le`, `char_gt`, `char_ge` are encoded directly; everything else on
characters unfolds through `ORD`, and `:string` stays an ordinary
`:char list`.  `ORD` and `CHR` are total only where the `:num` carrier
holds the code, so a goal over a large code needs `card num` above it.

Word arithmetic and character codes both read an atom's value from its
universe index, and binary integers would replace those bounds, so a
goal mentioning a word or a character disables binary integers rather
than losing the bounds it depends on; forcing them with
`upd_binary_ints (SOME true)` refuses the word or character operation
instead.  A typedef over `:num` or `:int` disables them for a different
reason: binarized, its bound is a numeral needing a carrier atom of its
own, and the scopes where the abstract type is large enough are exactly
the ones where that numeral is missing.

#### Types the executable backends can test

Quickcheck reaches `:rat` and `:real` by a different, independent route
from the model finder's Frac encoding: a built-in generator for each,
plus a computeLib fragment that decides closed literals directly.

The `:rat` fragment decides closed `:rat` arithmetic and comparison
literals -- except division by a zero literal and `rat_minv` at zero,
which it declines rather than mis-decides, matching `/`'s own
zero-divisor refusal.  It reduces a comparison to `T` or `F`, and an
arithmetic term to a raw `abs_rat (abs_frac (n, d))` value that is not
reduced to lowest terms.  The `:real` generator is registered the same
way; `:real` arithmetic and comparisons were already decided on closed
literals by `realLib` -- except `n / &0` for a nonzero `n`, left stuck
-- so Refute only adds `inv`, declining the same way at zero, and
otherwise decides to `real_of_num`/`real_neg`/`real_div` of numerals,
the same shape `realLib` itself already produces.  Both fragments are
added to the global compset, so they extend the ambient `EVAL` as noted
under "Getting started".

Because these fragments only *decide* a goal, never sample it, a closed
`:rat`/`:real` goal they reduce to `T` can soundly report
`NoCounterexample`: reducing a closed goal to `T` is a proof, and
nothing is quantified for a search to have missed.  A goal quantified
over `:rat` or `:real` variables can never license `NoCounterexample`
from Quickcheck: a finite sample never exhausts an infinite type, so
exhaustive and random search alike report an inconclusive result,
however large the size or count.

Finite maps (`:'a |-> 'b`) get the same asymmetry from Quickcheck
through a different mechanism: a `register_generator_family`
registration built from `FEMPTY`/`FUPDATE` fires on demand at whatever
concrete instance a goal needs, always `exhaustive = false` -- the flag
is fixed once for the type operator as a whole rather than computed per
monomorphic instance, so no key type, `:bool |-> bool` included, can
make the registration exhaustible.  The generator lives only on the
`Compute` substrate -- `NativeSML` and `Cv` each decline it by name and
fall through under `Auto`, since fmap's `TypeBase` entry has no
constructors of its own for either to build a native or a cv
representation from -- and exhaustive enumeration never fires for it
either, so on this route a closed fmap fact can be decided but a goal
quantified over an fmap variable can never license `NoCounterexample`.
The model finder reaches finite maps independently, through its own
typedef encoding, and can decide a quantified fmap goal outright when
the key type is exhaustively covered by the chosen scope.

### Smart testing

Refute rewrites executable bounded quantifiers before planning.  It
supports the universal forms `!n. n < e ==> P n`, `!n. n <= e ==> P n`,
`!n. n IN count e ==> P n`, `!x. MEM x xs ==> P x`, and the offset
interval `!n. lo <= n /\ n < hi ==> P n` (with either conjunct order and
either bound strict or non-strict), and their existential conjunction
duals.  The rewrites use `EVERY` or `EXISTS`, so a bound such as
`LENGTH xs` may depend on another generated value.  Unrestricted
quantifiers over infinite types do not become executable QC goals.  The
enumeration is materialised, so a very wide interval, such as
`0 <= n /\ n < 1000000`, costs proportionally to its length.

With `smart_quantifier = true`, premises are considered while the test
plan is built rather than after every variable has been generated.
Equality premises can bind variables directly.  With
`smart_generators = true`, a positive, first-order Horn premise recovered
from `Hol_reln`, or from Horn-shaped Boolean function equations, can be
mode-compiled to an `Enum` plan; a *negated* such premise can instead be
mode-compiled to a complement `Guard`, testing against the relation's
semantic complement rather than falling back to opaque, unconstrained
treatment of the relation.  A complement is only available for a mode
every clause admits: fully-input, no clause needing a `Generator` for a
variable outside the head, and no clause calling another relation.  That
last condition excludes every recursive relation, whose negated call
still falls back to opaque, unconstrained treatment; see the known-gaps
list below.  Unlike `Enum`, a complement needs no enumerator and
consumes no fuel: its condition is a closed guard over already-bound
inputs, decided the same way an ordinary guard is, so a plan reaching one
can still be exhausted exactly.  `depth` is the fixed fuel supplied to an
`Enum` enumerator; it has no effect on a complement.  Premises may be
reordered during mode analysis; `reorder_premises = false` disables that
step.  If mode compilation is not possible, Refute safely falls back to
generate-and-guard testing.

The practical difference is visible at small bounds.  At `size = 1` and
`depth = 8`, the Horn-function premise in the following example directly
generates a distinct list and finds a certified counterexample.  With
smart generators disabled, generate-and-test does not reach one at the
same bound and reports an incomplete no-hit result.

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- g `ALL_DISTINCT (xs : bool list) ==> LENGTH xs < 1`;
(* goal display omitted *)

- e (REFUTE_TAC_WITH
       [upd_search (Only [Exhaustive]),
        upd_substrate Compute,
        upd_sequential true,
        upd_size 1,
        upd_depth 8]);
(* temporary compilation messages omitted *)
> Refute found a counterexample
    (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 1, 0.011s,
     candidates generated 2, assumptions satisfied 2,
     conclusions evaluated 2):
    xs = [T]
  Certified: |- ~!xs. ALL_DISTINCT xs ==> LENGTH xs < 1
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - g `ALL_DISTINCT (xs : bool list) ==> LENGTH xs < 1`;
    (* goal display omitted *)

    - e (REFUTE_TAC_WITH
           [upd_search (Only [Exhaustive]),
            upd_substrate Compute,
            upd_sequential true,
            upd_size 1,
            upd_depth 8]);
    (* temporary compilation messages omitted *)
    > Refute found a counterexample
        (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 1, 0.011s,
         candidates generated 2, assumptions satisfied 2,
         conclusions evaluated 2):
        xs = [T]
      Certified: ⊢ ¬∀xs. ALL_DISTINCT xs ⇒ LENGTH xs < 1
```

Smart-enumerator fuel bounds every generated derivation.  Exhausting an
`Enum` or smart guard is consequently incomplete even if its finite
search happened not to find a hit.

### Certification and certainty

Execution substrates, generated SML, Kodkodi, and SAT solvers are
untrusted accelerators.  With the default `certify = true`, a Quickcheck
candidate is re-evaluated independently with `computeLib`.  A replay that
proves the instantiated proposition true discards the candidate; one that
proves it false attaches a theorem for the negated, closed instance.  If
direct evaluation is stuck, ordinary Quickcheck certification may normalize
the closure to PNF and replay an all-universal prefix using the candidate's
exact generated bindings.  This fallback remains partial for mixed or
existential PNF prefixes.  An inconclusive attempt keeps the
substrate-derived certainty with no theorem.  Certification is monotone:
it only discards false positives or adds theorems, and never weakens a
verdict.  Narrowing
grounds partial hits before evaluation and certifies PNF existential hits
by replaying their finite case tree.  Refute never adds an oracle tag.

Every counterexample records a `certainty` and, separately, whether a
theorem was built.  The two deliberately answer different questions, and
neither one determines the other.  `certainty` is the semantic strength
of the result: `Genuine` says that the backend's semantic conditions for
a real counterexample hold, while `QuasiGenuine` and `Potential` carry
the reasons they fall short.  A `Certified:` line says only that Refute
additionally constructed a HOL theorem.  For the model finder the
certainty is decided by the encoding, not by the certificate: Refute
trusts Kodkodi exactly as Isabelle/HOL's Nitpick trusts Kodkod, so a
translation that is sound and exact yields `Genuine`, one resting on an
approximation yields `QuasiGenuine`, and an unsound one yields
`Potential`.  The model finder additionally attempts a fail-closed
kernel replay of reconstructed values; when that replay cannot close the
negated goal, the result remains `Genuine` with no certificate, exactly
as for an uncertified QC hit below.
The backend and substrate fields make clear whether a report came from
the model finder, narrowing, or executable QC.

Setting `certify = false` skips all QC EVAL and replay certification.  A
QC hit remains `Genuine`, but gets no theorem and an explicit
uncertified marker:

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- the_config := (!the_config |> upd_certify false);
> val it = () : unit

- g `(n : num) < 3`;
(* goal display omitted *)

- e REFUTE_TAC;
> Refute found a counterexample
    (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 3, 0.0s,
     candidates generated 9, assumptions satisfied 9,
     conclusions evaluated 9):
    n = 3
  Certification: uncertified
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - the_config := (!the_config |> upd_certify false);
    > val it = () : unit

    - g `(n : num) < 3`;
    (* goal display omitted *)

    - e REFUTE_TAC;
    > Refute found a counterexample
        (backend: exhaustive, substrate: compute, size 3, 0.0s,
         candidates generated 9, assumptions satisfied 9,
         conclusions evaluated 9):
        n = 3
      Certification: uncertified
```

This opt-out is useful while exploring, but a client that requires a
kernel object must inspect the counterexample's `cert` field, not merely
test `certainty = Genuine`.

### Finding unused assumptions

Refute can also probe existing theorems for premises that may be
unnecessary.  This interface is SML-level rather than tactic-level, and
has three entry points:

```sml
check_unused_assms :
  config option -> string * thm -> string * int list list option
find_unused_assms :
  config option -> string -> (string * int list list option) list
print_unused_assms : config option -> string option -> unit
```

`NONE` derives a QC-only configuration from `!the_config`; `SOME cfg`
preserves the supplied backend selection.  Each probe nevertheless forces
sequential, quiet, abort-potential, expectation-free operation.  Probes
are serial.  An inconclusive result is conservatively treated as "not
shown unused" rather than as evidence that an assumption can be removed.

For each theorem, the tool strips outer universal quantifiers and indexes
the left-to-right chain of implication premises exactly as written.  It
does not split a conjunctive antecedent: in `(p /\ q) ==> r`, `p /\ q` is
one premise at index 0.  The tool first tests every single indexed premise,
then grows combinations only from individually droppable premises and
returns the maximal simultaneously droppable sets.  Result indexes are
zero-based; `print_unused_assms` displays them one-based.  `NONE` in the
per-theorem result means that the theorem has no implication premises,
whereas `SOME []` means that no premise was shown droppable.

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- val th = prove (``!p q. p ==> q ==> q``, simp []);
> val th = |- !p q. p ==> q ==> q : thm

- Refute.check_unused_assms NONE ("demo", th);
> val it = ("demo", SOME [[0]])
    : string * int list list option
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - val th = prove (``!p q. p ==> q ==> q``, simp []);
    > val th = ⊢ ∀p q. p ⇒ q ⇒ q : thm

    - Refute.check_unused_assms NONE ("demo", th);
    > val it = ("demo", SOME [[0]])
        : string * int list list option
```

`find_unused_assms cfgopt "foo"` checks all theorems returned by
`DB.thms "foo"`, sorted by name.  `print_unused_assms cfgopt NONE` checks
the current theory and prints a summary, including the number of
inconclusive probes.  Only a counterexample is decisive and the default
bounds deepen adaptively, so a probe that misses normally runs until the
whole-call `timeout` expires; supply `SOME cfg` with a smaller timeout
to pace a sweep of a large theory.

### Configuration reference

Configurations are immutable records changed by updater functions.  The
usual idiom is:

```sml
val cfg = Refute.default_config
  |> Refute.upd_timeout 5.0
  |> Refute.upd_search
       (Refute.Only [Refute.Exhaustive, Refute.Narrowing])
  |> Refute.upd_size 7
```

Prefer `REFUTE_TAC_WITH` for a one-off tactic call; mutate `the_config`
only when changing persistent interactive defaults.  The common fields
are:

| Updater | Default | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `upd_timeout` | `10.0` | Seconds in the whole-call search deadline. |
| `upd_search` | `AllBackends` | Typed selection of all, Quickcheck, or explicit backends. |
| `upd_sequential` | `false` | Run backends by weight instead of racing them. |
| `upd_genuine_only` | `false` | Suppress potential results and continue looking for genuine ones. |
| `upd_abort_potential` | `false` | Stop at the first counterexample regardless of certainty. |
| `upd_quiet` | `false` | Suppress Refute traces, messages, and warnings for this call. |
| `upd_no_assms` | `false` | Ignore the goal's assumptions. |
| `upd_evals` | `[]` | Extra terms whose values should be reconstructed and displayed. |
| `upd_expect` | `NoExpectation` | Regression expectation checked after the run. |
| `upd_max_counterexamples` | `1` | Maximum QC counterexamples requested. |
| `upd_tag` | `""` | Text appended to the formatted outcome. |
| `upd_widths` | `[1,2,3,4]` | Widths tried for a word type variable; entries lie between 1 and 32. |

#### Quickcheck and narrowing fields

| Updater | Default | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `upd_size` | `10` | Maximum generated size and narrowing refinement depth; pins a fixed bound. |
| `upd_iterative_size` | default mode | Initial window for adaptive iterative deepening. |
| `upd_iterations` | `100` | Random draws. |
| `upd_depth` | `10` | Fixed smart-enumerator fuel for each plan. |
| `upd_finite_types` | `true` | Add small fresh finite types when monomorphizing type variables. |
| `upd_finite_type_size` | `3` | Number of such finite cardinalities considered. |
| `upd_default_type` | `[``:num``]` | Type instances used when `finite_types` is false. |
| `upd_instantiate` | `[]` | Per-type-variable pins, overriding `finite_types`/`default_type`. |
| `upd_substrate` | `Auto` | `Auto`, `NativeSML`, `Cv`, or `Compute` for executable QC. |
| `upd_allow_existentials` | `true` | Permit PNF narrowing of prenex existentials. |
| `upd_finite_functions` | `true` | Finitize narrowing function inputs as update chains or constants. |
| `upd_certify` | `true` | Independently certify QC and narrowing hits. |
| `upd_smart_quantifier` | `true` | Compile premises into binds, guards, and generators. |
| `upd_smart_generators` | `true` | Enable Horn relation/function `Enum` plans. |
| `upd_optimise_equality` | `true` | Turn suitable equality premises into direct bindings. |
| `upd_reorder_premises` | `true` | Reorder premises during smart mode inference. |
| `upd_use_subtype` | `false` | Test a generator-less typedef by transporting to its representation type. |
| `upd_allow_function_inversion` | `false` | Invert an `f ... = res` premise via its graph clauses. |
| `upd_seed` | `NONE` | Reproducible random seed; `NONE` advances a deterministic session stream. |

The default size mode is iterative deepening: `10` is an initial window,
not a terminal bound, and testing deepens until the deadline.  `upd_size`
deliberately pins a fixed, reproducible bound, while `upd_iterative_size`
sets a different initial window and restores adaptive deepening.  The
separate `depth` field bounds narrowing; it is not a second
0-through-depth schedule loop.

By default, QC substitutes one type for *every* type variable in a goal
at once: a goal with two type variables is tested with both at the same
type, and each report names the one cardinality that instance tested.
`upd_instantiate` pins named type variables to fixed types instead: a
`SOME ty` key pins the named type variable, and a variable no entry
reaches still takes the shared carrier/width, exactly as `[]` would.  A
`NONE` key is the fallback for every *non-width* variable no `SOME`
entry names, so a `NONE`-only assignment pins all of them and, like a
fully-`SOME` assignment, collapses the search to one instance -- unless
the goal has a symbolic word width, since a width variable takes a pin
only from its own `SOME` entry (`NONE` never reaches it: carriers and
word-width numerals are disjoint value spaces, so a single fallback
cannot serve both) and otherwise keeps varying over the configured
widths.  A type variable in a word's index position must therefore be
pinned to a concrete word-width type (`` `:1` ``, `` `:2` ``, ...), not
an ordinary carrier.  Naming a type variable the goal does not have,
naming a width variable with a non-width type, or naming a pin value
that still carries type variables of its own, is a configuration error,
reported as an inconclusive result rather than silently ignored.
Because pinning only changes which monomorphic proxies QC tests, not
what the original polymorphic goal means, a completely covered but
still-polymorphic goal stays inconclusive exactly as it does without any
pins -- `upd_instantiate` can turn an inconclusive result into a
`Genuine` counterexample, but it can never turn a polymorphic goal's
exhaustible search into `NoCounterexample`.  `upd_instantiate` scopes to
QC and narrowing; the model finder is unaffected and has its own
per-type-variable updater, `upd_card [(SOME ty, [n])]`.

A free variable at a typedef type `t` (`new_type_definition`, abstraction
`abs`, representation type `rty`, characteristic predicate `P`) usually
leaves QC with nothing to test: `t` has no generator and none of the
built-in fallbacks apply.  `upd_use_subtype true` rewrites `!x : t. B x`
to `!r : rty. P r ==> B (abs r)`, contracting every `rep (abs r)` redex
the substitution creates back to `r`, and reports a hit's binding for
`x` as `abs`-of-the-tested `r`, never `r` itself.  `TYPE_DEFINITION P
rep` makes `abs` restricted to `{r | P r}` a bijection onto `t`, so the
guard is the soundness condition, not an optimisation: dropping it lets
a representation value outside `t`'s image manufacture a spurious
refutation.  Whether a filtered representation search counts as
exhausted follows the same completeness bookkeeping as any other QC
search; transport adds no bespoke exhaustion rule in either direction.
In practice that makes transport a counterexample-search aid rather
than a verification route: the generator ranges over the whole
representation type and does not know the characteristic predicate
bounds it, so a true transported goal stays inconclusive unless its body
collapses to a ground truth under normalization.
A genuine transported hit never certifies: replaying the original
closure needs the untransported `x`, but a transported search's
environment only ever binds the representation-typed `r`, so the
report always carries `Genuine` certainty with no attached certificate
-- proving it by kernel replay would require evaluating the very
`rep (abs r)` redex the contraction exists to avoid.
Transport is a fallback of last resort: a registered generator or an
`abstract_generator` registration for `t` wins, a TypeBase datatype for
`t` wins, and only when none of those apply and `t` is itself a
harvestable typedef with a real `TYPE_DEFINITION` theorem behind it
does the flag take effect.  That last requirement excludes the model
finder's synthetic frac and fmap typedef entries, which are an
encoding device for the model finder and carry no such theorem, so
nothing licenses the guard or the contraction for them.  It also does not reach
inside another type: a free variable of type `t list` keeps today's
generator-less, inconclusive behaviour, since transporting it would
require lifting the predicate and generator through the list structure,
which this fallback does not do.  Default `false`, matching Isabelle's
`use_subtype`; the model finder never sees the rewrite; it already
handles typedefs natively.
Deciding whether `t` is a harvestable typedef triggers the model
finder's own lazy harvest, so a `use_subtype`-enabled QC-only search
that reaches this fallback permanently populates the model finder's
global typedef registry -- the same harvest the model finder performs
on its own, hence idempotent and not a soundness concern, but an
observable side effect of what looks like a QC-only call.

#### Model-finder fields

Several MF options are rows.  A `NONE` key is the fallback;
`SOME ty` or `SOME tm` overrides matching types or constants.  A Boolean
row value of `NONE` asks Refute to choose, while `SOME true` or
`SOME false` forces the choice.

| Updater | Default | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| `upd_card` | fallback `[1,...,10]` | Candidate finite cardinalities by type; pins fixed rows. |
| `upd_iterative_card` | default mode | Initial adaptive cardinality windows. |
| `upd_max` | fallback `[~1]` | Maximum constructor multiplicities; `~1` means automatic. |
| `upd_mono` | fallback `NONE` | Automatic or forced monotonicity classification by type. |
| `upd_wf` | fallback `NONE` | Automatic or forced well-founded treatment by constant. |
| `upd_sat_solver` | `"smart"` | Kodkodi SAT solver selection. |
| `upd_batch_size` | `50` | Scope problems submitted per solver batch. |
| `upd_falsify` | `true` | Falsify the conjecture; false searches for a model of it. |
| `upd_user_axioms` | `NONE` | Automatic or forced inclusion of user axioms. |
| `upd_destroy_constrs` | `true` | Simplify constructor applications during preprocessing. |
| `upd_total_consts` | `NONE` | Automatic or forced total relational encoding of constants. |
| `upd_peephole_optim` | `true` | Enable relational peephole optimization. |
| `upd_datatype_sym_break` | `5` | Datatype symmetry-breaking strength. |
| `upd_kodkod_sym_break` | `15` | Kodkod's symmetry-breaking limit. |
| `upd_max_potential` | `1` | Global budget for potential models. |
| `upd_max_genuine` | `1` | Global budget for genuine models. |
| `upd_atoms` | fallback `[]` | Preferred printed atom names by type. |
| `upd_format` | fallback `[1]` | Group curried model-function arguments into tuples. |
| `upd_show_types` | `false` | Include interpreted type universes in models. |
| `upd_show_skolems` | `true` | Include Skolem values. |
| `upd_show_consts` | `false` | Include reconstructed constant tables. |
| `upd_debug` | `false` | Enable MF debug mode and single-problem batches. |
| `upd_overlord` | `false` | Keep solver files with stable names in the current directory. |
| `upd_max_threads` | `0` | Kodkodi thread cap; zero leaves it unspecified. |
| `upd_tac_timeout` | `0.5` | Seconds for inner monotonicity and well-foundedness tactics. |
| `upd_specialize` | `true` | Specialize a constant at its equation-invariant arguments. |
| `upd_box` | fallback `NONE` | Automatically or forcibly box function and pair types. |
| `upd_binary_ints` | `NONE` | Automatically or forcibly use binary integers. |
| `upd_bits` | `[1,...,10]` | Candidate binary word widths. |
| `upd_star_linear_preds` | `true` | Use exact closure for linear inductive predicates. |
| `upd_iter` | fallback depth row | Fixpoint-unrolling depths for inductive predicates. |
| `upd_bisim_depth` | `[9]` | Codatatype bisimulation depths; `~1` disables bisimulation. |
| `upd_finitize` | fallback `NONE` | Automatically or forcibly finitize shallow infinite types. |
| `upd_whack` | `[]` | Constants whose definitions preprocessing should ignore. |
| `upd_need` | `NONE` | Optional terms whose values must occur in a model. |
| `upd_merge_type_vars` | `false` | Merge all MF type-variable scopes into the first variable. |

Card rows are iterative by default: the fallback `[1,...,10]` is an
initial window that keeps growing until the deadline.  `upd_card` pins
fixed rows, while `upd_iterative_card` restores adaptive scanning; an
iterative row must be a consecutive prefix `[1, ..., n]`.

The default iterator row is `[0,1,2,4,8,12,16,20,24,28]`.  A mutual
fixpoint group shares one row: members are considered in cases-theorem
order, and the first explicit row wins.  Requesting multiple models may
force an incremental SAT solver.  Merging type variables reduces scope
products but can lose models that require different cardinalities for
the original variables.

### The SML interface

Tools that consume a search's result programmatically, rather than
reading a printed report, call Refute directly.  The principal result
type is:

```sml
datatype outcome =
    Counterexample of counterexample list
  | NoCounterexample
  | Model of counterexample list
  | NoModel
  | Unknown of string list
```

`Unknown` is what the tactics print as "Refute search was inconclusive";
its string list is the reason list shown there.  It carries timeout,
inapplicability, missing-generator, bounds-relative, and other
incomplete-search reasons.  `Model` and `NoModel` are the distinct
results of a `upd_falsify false` model search.

A `counterexample` records its `backend`, execution `substrate`,
`certainty`, free-variable `bindings`, requested `evals`, optional
`cert`, optional finite `scope` and model, and `stats`.  The `bindings`
field holds the variable assignments the report prints, and `cert` holds
the theorem behind a `Certified:` line, so a caller need not parse
Refute's printed text.

The public entry points are:

| Entry point | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| `refute cfg tm` | Search for a counterexample to `tm` with `cfg`, print the result unless quiet, and return the structured `outcome`. |
| `refute_with updates tm` | Apply `updates` to `!the_config`, then search `tm`. |
| `refute_goal cfg (asms, tm)` | Search the goal whose assumptions are `asms` and conclusion is `tm`. |
| `refute_goal_with updates goal` | The goal form of `refute_with`. |
| `quickcheck tm` | Use `!the_config`, but select only the Quickcheck backends `exhaustive`, `random`, and `narrowing`. |
| `model_refute tm` | Use `!the_config`, but select only the `kodkod` model-finder backend. |
| `try_refute cfg goal` | Run quietly and return `SOME (backend, outcome)` only for a counterexample, or `NONE` for a miss, timeout, or inconclusive search. |
| `find_unused_assms cfgopt thy` | Probe the named theory and return possible unused-assumption sets. |

There are also conveniences `refute_def tm`, which calls `refute
!the_config tm`, and `refute_top ()`, which applies `refute_goal` to the
current top goal.  `apply_updates updates cfg` constructs an exact
configuration, while `current_config updates` applies the same list to
the interactive configuration; these are the functions the two
configurable tactics are built from.

`try_refute` is intended for other automation.  It preserves the
caller's backend selection but forces sequential execution, seed 42,
`NoExpectation`, and quiet mode.  For example, this call prints no
Refute report:

```{=latex}
\begin{session}
\begin{verbatim}
- Option.map #1
    (try_refute
       (default_config
          |> upd_search (Only [Exhaustive])
          |> upd_substrate Compute
          |> upd_size 4)
       ([], ``(n : num) < 3``));
> val it = SOME "exhaustive" : string option
\end{verbatim}
\end{session}
```

```{=mdbook}
    - Option.map #1
        (try_refute
           (default_config
              |> upd_search (Only [Exhaustive])
              |> upd_substrate Compute
              |> upd_size 4)
           ([], ``(n : num) < 3``));
    > val it = SOME "exhaustive" : string option
```

### Extending Refute

All registrations below are session-local ML state.  They do not define
HOL constants or types, and a later registration for the same key
replaces the old one.

* `register_generator ty {enumerate, random}` installs a QC generator.
  `enumerate` has type `int -> term list`; `random` has type
  `int -> rng -> term * rng`.  At least one callback must be present.
  `abstract_generator {ty, constructors, pred}` instead describes an
  abstract type through existing constructor terms and an optional
  predicate; it is an incomplete registration, so it can find
  counterexamples but never establishes `NoCounterexample`.
* `register_generator_family {tyop, constructors, canonical}` registers a
  QC generator for every concrete instance of a type operator at once,
  built from constructor constants whose declared result type is `tyop`
  applied to distinct type variables (e.g. `FEMPTY`/`FUPDATE` for
  `:'a |-> 'b`).  Unlike `register_generator`, nothing is stored per
  instance: `tyop` is matched on demand, so this fires for a type never
  previously seen, and the resulting generator always has
  `exhaustive = false`.  `canonical`, if present, is a display-only
  `term_postprocessor`, fed as a callback into the same registry-consulting
  walk `register_term_postprocessor` below drives, so it composes with
  every other registered postprocessor rather than running as a second,
  separate walk; it applies to a reported binding at that type after
  testing and certification have already run against the raw term; it
  removes structure a later part of the same candidate has overwritten,
  and need not identify every pair of terms that denote the same value.
  Registration fails if `tyop` already has `TypeBase` datatype
  constructors, the same precedence `register_codatatype` enforces.
* `register_substrate {name, priority, accepts, preflight, compile}`
  extends or replaces the executable substrate ladder.  A substrate must
  compile the common plan and preserve candidate order and cleanup;
  returning `Inapplicable` allows `Auto` to continue.
* `register_backend backend` adds or replaces a weighted backend.
  `register_backend_with_ceiling backend ceiling` additionally declares
  a configuration- and instance-sensitive certainty ceiling.  The
  callback must overestimate: an underestimate can stop a race before a
  stronger result and violates the contract.
* `register_codatatype {tyop, case_const, constructors, witness}` validates
  the shape of `tyop`, `case_const` and `constructors` (non-empty, distinct
  constructors, each declaring - at its own declared type, not merely at
  whatever instance the caller applied it - a `tyop`-headed result type; a
  case constant with a unique valid scrutinee position, in any argument;
  the type applied to distinct type variables) and registers.  When `tyop`
  is a type the datatype database already knows as a free datatype, every
  registered constructor must also be one of that type's database
  constructors, by key, rejecting an impostor constant declared at the
  right type without needing a witness; for a type the database does not
  know, this imposes nothing.  Beyond that, shape validation cannot tell
  codata from an ordinary inductive datatype - constructor and case types
  look the same either way - so `witness = NONE` accepts the registration
  on trust, exactly as Isabelle's own `register_codatatype` does: a wrong
  registration removes the type's acyclicity constraints and can produce a
  wrong model.  The built-in registrations below are trusted this same
  way too, but through a different path: their lazy harvest calls the
  shape check directly rather than `register_codatatype`, so no witness
  ever applies to them.  `witness = SOME thm` narrows what a caller can
  get away with further: after stripping `thm`'s outer `!`s, its
  conclusion must be `?x. body`, `body` an equation with one side `aconv`
  to `x` (either orientation) and the other a constructor application
  `C a1 ... an`, where `C`'s key matches a registered constructor and `x`
  is `aconv` to some argument reached by walking `C`'s constructor spine -
  descending into an argument only when its own head is again a
  registered constructor - not merely free somewhere in it.  Every
  registered constructor's *declared* result type is `tyop`-headed, so
  `C`'s key match makes it literally that declared constant; `C` need not
  be fully applied, so `x`'s own type need not be `tyop`-headed.
  Mutual-cycle witnesses (`?x y. x = C ... y ... /\ y = C ... x ...`) and
  HOL4's per-type bisimulation theorems (`llist_bisimulation` and
  friends) are not of this shape and are not accepted; a caller holding
  one passes `witness = NONE`.  Together, the witness rules out generic
  shape defeats and the database cross-check rules out impostor
  constants for types the database knows; beyond that, the constructor
  list remains the caller's assertion.  Built-in lazy registrations
  cover `llist`, `ltree`, `itree`, `itreeTau`, `lbtree`, and `path` when
  their theories are present.
* `register_typedef {ty, abs, rep, absrep_thms}` registers existing Abs and
  Rep constants.  `absrep_thms` is either the exported bijection conjunction
  as a singleton list, or its two conjuncts `!a. abs (rep a) = a` and
  `!r. P r = (rep (abs r) = r)` as separate theorems in either order; both
  forms register identically.
  `register_quotient {qty, rty, abs, rep, equiv_thm}` registers an
  existing quotient.  `equiv_thm` must be hypothesis-free and either a
  `QUOTIENT equiv abs rep` theorem mentioning the registered `abs` and
  `rep`, or a total equivalence `!x y. R x y <=> (R x = R y)`.  Its shape
  selects the encoding: a `QUOTIENT` theorem gets the sound partial
  encoding, whereas a total equivalence drops its then redundant domain
  axiom.
* `register_frac_type {tyop, ersatz}` supplies a fraction representation
  and replacements.  `register_frac_type_rat ()` and
  `register_frac_type_real ()` idempotently restore the built-in
  rational and `real` registrations, respectively; both are installed by
  default on the same Frac carrier.  `register_ersatz` adds an
  individual original-to-replacement constant mapping.
* `register_term_postprocessor pattern f` installs a bottom-up,
  display-only model transformation.  Matching transformations compose
  from general to specific, then with any `canonical` callback a
  generator family registered (`register_generator_family` above);
  failures or type changes leave the raw term unchanged.  Certification
  always uses raw terms.  `lookup_term_postprocessor ty` returns the
  composed callback.  This is the one walk both the model finder's own
  display and a QC-strategy counterexample's reported binding go
  through.  Two patterns are installed by default, each with its own
  idempotent restore/refresh entry point mirroring the Frac pair above:
  `register_function_display` dedups a function-type witness's update
  chain at `:'a -> 'b`, and `register_fmap_display` unwraps the model
  finder's finite-map representation into FUPDATE notation at
  `:'a |-> 'b`.  Registering at exactly one of those patterns replaces
  the built-in, since registration matches on the pattern; unlike the
  Frac pair, this has no automatic re-installation, so the matching
  entry point is the way back.

The theorem sets used by MF preprocessing can be extended in a theory
script with `export_refute_simp`, `export_refute_psimp`, and
`export_refute_unfold`.  Their arguments are names of existing theorems
to export into the corresponding set.

Structural registrations require distinct type variables as parameters
and existing constants for constructors, case operators, Abs, and Rep.
The model finder also attempts a lazy, validated database harvest when it
first encounters an unregistered typedef or quotient; explicit
registration remains useful when automatic harvesting cannot recover an
otherwise standard `QUOTIENT` bridge.  Typedef harvesting accepts either
the exported bijection conjunction or its two conjuncts saved separately,
provided both live in the same theory; halves split across two theories
are not paired.  `harvest_registrations ()` is an
opt-in alternative that sweeps every theory in the session's ancestry
once, oldest first, running that same lazy harvest against every type
operator those theories declare instead of waiting for a goal to demand
it; it returns the typedef and quotient types it newly registered, so an
immediate repeat call reports nothing further.  The default remains the
demand-driven lazy harvest.

### Installing Kodkodi

The three native QC backends need no external solver.  The `kodkod`
backend is Unix-only and requires Java, the standard Unix `setsid` utility,
and Kodkodi 1.5.7.  `setsid` isolates the launched process group so Refute
can reliably terminate it.  Obtain
`kodkodi-1.5.7.tar.gz` from the Isabelle components mirror at
<https://isabelle.in.tum.de/components/>, unpack it, and set
`HOL4_KODKODI` to the component directory.  Its `jar` directory must
contain:

* `antlr-runtime-3.1.1.jar`;
* `kodkod-1.5.jar`;
* `kodkodi-1.5.7.jar`; and
* `sat4j-2.3.jar`.

Java is selected from `HOL4_JAVA_EXECUTABLE`, then `JAVA_HOME/bin/java`,
then `java` on `PATH`.  As an escape hatch,
`HOL4_KODKODI_EXEC` may name a complete standalone launcher command; it
takes precedence and is passed to the shell verbatim.  The component's
usual launcher depends on Isabelle and is not a standalone replacement.
Configuration probes are lazy and never start Java merely by loading
Refute.  Without a configured model finder, `MODEL_REFUTE_TAC` reports
an inconclusive search and still leaves the goal unchanged.

SAT4J is the portable default.  JNI MiniSat, CryptoMiniSat, and Lingeling
are advertised only when their respective shared libraries are found in
the component's platform `jni` directory.  External solver environment
variables understood by Kodkodi, such as `MINISAT_HOME`,
`CRYPTOMINISAT_HOME`, `ZCHAFF_HOME`, `RSAT_HOME`, and `RISS3G_HOME`, are
retained.

### Limitations and interpretation

Counterexample search is bounded and incomplete.  Three of the
limitations below are inherited from the approach itself, not
shortfalls still to close -- Isabelle's Nitpick has the same three.  In
particular:

* refuting `?x. P x` means showing that *no* `x` satisfies `P`; no
  finite search can show that over an infinite type, so a goal whose
  falsity turns on such an existential never earns a genuine
  counterexample and never `NoCounterexample`: by default the search is
  inconclusive, and a model finder run at explicitly bounded scopes
  reports at most a `Potential` counterexample, drawn from the liberal
  problem.  An existential in the
  *witness* direction -- finding one, the ordinary case -- is
  unaffected;
* every value the model finder can assign to a `real` variable is a
  rational number (see the fraction encoding above), so a proposition
  false only at an irrational point has no countermodel Refute can
  express: the search exhausts its scopes and reports an inconclusive
  result instead of the counterexample that exists;
* a goal that remains polymorphic after every configured instantiation
  degrades a would-be decisive answer to an inconclusive one, on both
  engines, even when its tested instances are all covered: neither
  engine reports `NoCounterexample` for a claim it verified only at
  chosen monomorphic proxies, since that would report something stronger
  than what was actually established;
* random exhaustion, smart-generator fuel exhaustion, and narrowing at a
  finite depth normally produce an inconclusive report, not a proof of
  validity;
* ordinary QC requires executable terms and generators.  Unrestricted
  quantifiers over infinite types, choice, and unsupported constants may
  make a substrate inapplicable.  A datatype recursive beneath a function
  type (e.g. an interaction tree's `Vis`) cannot be exhaustively
  enumerated and so is refused under exhaustive QC and narrowing; Cv is
  also inapplicable to it, though for a broader, pre-existing reason
  (nested recursive datatype generators in general, not this shape
  specifically).  Only native and compute random QC can still test such
  a type, by drawing a random function value on demand, mirroring
  Isabelle's `random_fun_lift`; when a value of the type is instead
  pinned by an equation the search cannot evaluate, the search prunes
  that case rather than generating it.  The random function generator
  also samples its points eagerly, so its cost grows with the number of
  function-typed values it must produce even though the depth of any one
  value stays bounded; Isabelle's version is lazy and pays only for
  points the test actually reaches;
* smart generators support positive first-order modes, and negated
  first-order modes whose relation SmartGen can classify (a complement
  `Guard`, via the relation's semantic complement).  Classification
  excludes every recursive relation: a self- or mutually-recursive
  relation's negated call falls back to an opaque guard, not a
  complement.  Giving it a bounded complement with a downgraded verdict,
  instead of admitting it outright, remains an open gap.  A higher-order
  relation parameter applied to a statically known closed predicate is
  specialised -- substituted through the relation's clauses before mode
  inference -- and can then compile to a positive-mode enumerator like
  any first-order relation; the same parameter left as a runtime-bound
  variable, other un-classifiable negative modes, and general function
  flattening still fall back to guards rather than becoming errors;
* subtype lifting does not reach inside another type: a free variable
  of type `t list`, say, keeps today's generator-less, inconclusive
  behaviour even when `t` itself is a liftable typedef (see
  `upd_use_subtype` above);
* function inversion -- synthesising Horn clauses for an ordinary
  function's graph (`f a1 ... an = res`) from its own defining
  equations, and running mode inference over them so an inverting mode
  can drive a generator -- is implemented for a purely self-recursive
  definition.  A call to any *other* function refuses the whole function
  rather than approximating its graph: an under-approximating graph used
  as a generator would be unsound, so the goal instead falls back to
  ordinary `Gen`+`Guard` planning, or to the model finder -- a reach
  limitation of this route, not a soundness or a lost-goal one.  It is
  gated by `upd_allow_function_inversion` (default `false`).  On, a goal
  premise recognising `f a1 ... an = res` (either orientation, `f`
  applied at exactly its own maximal arity) can compile to an
  inverting `Enum`, competing on score with every other route exactly
  like an ordinary relational premise.  A position -- argument or
  result -- that is neither fully bound nor a bare unbound variable,
  e.g. `z :: zs` in `xs ++ ys = z :: zs`, is a pattern the mode system
  cannot express and refuses the whole premise; a negated graph
  premise always falls back to an opaque guard, since a graph
  relation's modes never enter the negative-mode table; an all-input
  premise (every position already bound) offers no inverting `Enum`
  either, because every position is Input and no mode survives with a
  non-empty output set, and is left to the ordinary route with no
  enumerator at all -- a premise whose function is not executable but
  whose graph clauses would invert, with every position already bound,
  is no longer reachable through inversion either way, though both
  routes forfeit completeness for such a premise regardless, so this
  costs refutation reach, not certainty; the flag is itself a
  smart-generator route, so it also requires `upd_smart_generators`
  (default on);
* narrowing finitizes function inputs.  The constant-function fallback
  for a higher-order domain is an approximation, and every depth bound
  remains incomplete;
* the model finder depends on finite scopes and on the soundness
  conditions reported in `certainty`.  Unsupported encodings are refused
  or downgraded rather than silently called genuine;
* fraction support covers `rat` and `real` by default; certification of
  a `real` Frac counterexample offers its genuine literal value to replay
  as an untrusted candidate, and a linear real-arithmetic leaf can then
  certify a goal computeLib alone cannot.  A nonlinear instance still
  comes back inconclusive, leaving the counterexample genuine but
  uncertified, which the trust model accepts
  rather than blocks; `rat` counterexamples are unaffected, since the
  candidate-hint mechanism is deliberately scoped to `real`-typed atoms
  by a structural check, and a `rat` Frac atom is the same kind of
  opaque reserved variable as a `real` one;
* a hand-written definition headed by `fixedPoint$lfp` or `fixedPoint$gfp`
  but absent from the `Hol_reln`/`Hol_coreln` registries is recognized if
  its monotonicity can be discharged automatically; a non-monotone
  functional falls back to plain `lfp_def`/`gfp_def` unfolding, which
  stays sound but is not refused; mutual hand-rolled fixpoints are
  recognized only as their single tupled predicate, not unrolled jointly;
* the model finder encodes machine words of concrete width only, refuses
  a width whose carrier would exceed 1024 atoms, and refuses
  width-changing and bit-permuting word operations by name;
* if lazy metadata harvest cannot validate an Abs/Rep type, the model
  finder reports an inconclusive result and asks for an explicit typedef
  registration;
* `relation$WF` is encoded through a builtin ersatz definition that is
  exact on a genuinely finite or polymorphic domain, but only `Potential`
  over an intrinsically infinite domain, where the encoding's finiteness
  disjunct can never be decided from a bounded scope;
* `pred_set$CARD` and `pred_set$SUM_IMAGE` are also encoded through
  builtin ersatz definitions, both built on the same
  guarded-Hilbert-choice witness list described below
  (`pred_set$SUM_SET`, being `SUM_IMAGE I`, reaches the latter through
  its own defining equation).  These rows are liberal, not exact: a
  missing witness list degrades a verdict instead of producing a
  spurious `Genuine` refutation, but also means `NoCounterexample` is
  never available through them, regardless of the element type's
  finiteness;
* `relation$WFREC` is encoded through a pair of builtin ersatz
  definitions that tie a literal recursive equation instead of
  unfolding HOL4's own Hilbert-choice fixpoint witness function.  The
  recursion is guarded by the well-foundedness premise `WF R`, and
  matches HOL's own characterization exactly wherever that premise is
  decided; off it, the value is `unknown`, honestly reflecting that
  nothing characterizes HOL's own witness there either.  That
  `unknown` sits at a value position, not a formula position, so any
  goal that reaches this encoding loses `NoCounterexample` outright,
  not merely `Potential` as with `WF` above: an unrepresented value is
  exactly as restrictive on the liberal side of a search as on the
  sound side, so an exhausted search can no longer be trusted to mean
  no counterexample exists;
* `:'a |-> 'b` (finite maps) get their own typedef, over representation
  type `'a -> 'b option` rather than the kernel's own (recursive, and
  not exactly representable at scope-search precision) `fmap_TY_DEF`.
  Its FINITE-guarded `unknown` escape is availability, not a soundness
  crutch: every value a scope ever assigns is already a finite relation
  regardless of which disjunct holds, so a `Genuine` verdict at an
  intrinsically infinite key type is exact.  Off-domain `FAPPLY` reads
  `unknown` rather than an arbitrary value.  At a non-`bool` range this
  `unknown` sits at a value position, so (as with `WFREC` above) *any*
  goal mentioning `FAPPLY` at that instance loses `NoCounterexample`
  outright, whether or not its truth actually depends on the off-domain
  read; at `bool` range the same `unknown` is a `Formula` and does not
  trigger that veto, so a genuinely true off-domain fact there is
  instead certified, or left `Potential`, through the ordinary
  sound/liberal problem split.  A finite map's models can also print raw
  representation structure -- an internal `refuteTheory` constant plus
  combinator-update notation, such as `fm = abs_fmap' (K ?)⦇0 ↦ NONE⦈`
  -- when the witness-generated `FEMPTY |+ ...` display cannot reconstruct
  a chain from the underlying representation (an unrepresented base or a
  duplicate key); and the typedef's registration carries its own
  bijection theorems as inverse axioms, the structurally correct thing
  for any typedef registration to supply, so that no goal can rely on
  `FLOOKUP` failing to be injective on the abstract carrier;
* Hilbert choice (`min$@`) is otherwise an uninterpreted function; a
  predicate with no in-scope witness would read an arbitrary value off it,
  so the model finder inserts a guard at every syntactic occurrence and
  reports the choice's value as unrepresented instead when no witness is
  in scope.  A later simplification can erase an inserted guard's `if`,
  but the encoding stays sound in that case too, and a fired guard's
  bounds signal (below) survives the erasure regardless.  Two escapes
  stand the guard down where the unguarded read is independently sound: a
  predicate provably empty in HOL (decided by a fixed, terminating
  decision procedure under a syntactic fragment and size gate, never a
  search), and a predicate over `bool`, always exactly represented.  A
  fired guard also blocks `NoCounterexample`: a search that finds no model
  reports a bounds-relative inconclusive result instead, since a
  truncated witness cannot be told apart from a genuinely empty one; and
* HolRefute is built only with Poly/ML.  Kodkodi adds the Unix, Java, and
  local-component restrictions described above.

A counterexample is useful evidence that a statement is false, especially
when it carries a certificate.  `NoCounterexample` is the stronger
diagnostic claim that the whole search space was covered, but it is not a
HOL proof object.  An inconclusive report is only guidance for changing
bounds, generators, backends, or the conjecture.

Worked, executable examples of all four tactics -- from first steps
through case studies adapted from Isabelle's Nitpick and Quickcheck
example suites -- live in `src/HolRefute/examples`, indexed by the
`README` there.

\index{HolRefute|)}
